                    clock_r4:               `Service night · the critic and the jury are seated`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Read the Pantry`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The draft menu is dazzling — and it runs food cost at <strong>41%</strong>, eight points over baseline and well past the <strong>35% floor</strong>. The three regenerative farms can guarantee only <strong>60%</strong> of volume, waste sits at <strong>9%</strong> against a 4% promise, and the panel flagged two dishes as <em>“worthy but joyless”</em>. Don't defend every creative dish, and don't strip the regeneration out to save margin. Find exactly where creativity and economics collide, then use Leone's lens: <strong>improvise the volatile-supply courses, structure the anchor dishes that protect the margin</strong>.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Source the Regenerative Menu`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Now make the sourcing calls under a <strong>60% supply ceiling</strong>. For each ingredient: commit to a regenerative farm (story value, higher cost, lower volume), blend openly with conventional, hide conventional behind the claim, or substitute an abundant local alternative. The award jury <em>audits your suppliers</em> — quietly blending conventional behind a “pasture-raised” claim is not margin, it is a greenwashing story in the regional press. Set a regenerative share you can honestly back, design a contingency for the heirloom bean that may not arrive, and spend to stabilise weekly farm volume.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Engineer the Menu`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Bring the menu inside the floor <em>without</em> killing novelty or breaking the waste target. Apply the <strong>stars / plowhorses / puzzles / dogs</strong> grid: promote high-margin stars, rework puzzles, cut the dogs. Use <strong>root-to-leaf</strong> cooking to turn the 9% waste into stock, ferments and garnish — that cuts the waste KPI <em>and</em> food cost in one move. Rework the two “joyless” dishes so they are craveable AND regenerative. And remember: price can carry some of the cost, but a sharp jump from €115 loses the regulars.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Launch & Defend the 12-Month Plan`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The critic and the sustainability jury are booked tonight. Show that regeneration is a <strong>repeatable operating model</strong>, not a one-night event. Decide how the menu <strong>flexes weekly</strong> with the volatile harvest — fixed anchors plus a weekly flex section let consistency and improvisation coexist. Decide how front-of-house tells the story <em>without lecturing</em> — the food leads, the story supports. Present all four KPIs measured and owned, and defend that the constraints sharpened the creativity rather than smothering it.`,

                    r1_tension_label:       `Where do creativity and economics actually collide on this menu?`,
                    tension_costnovelty:    `In the dishes that add cost without adding the novelty customers value — flag them dish by dish`,
                    tension_allcreative:    `Nowhere real — the draft is brilliant, just defend every creative dish as-is`,
                    tension_cutregen:       `Everywhere regenerative — strip the costly regenerative ingredients out to protect margin`,
                    tension_priceonly:      `It is purely a pricing gap — raise the check and the tension disappears`,
                    r1_split_label:         `Using Leone's lens, how do you split the menu between improvised and structured?`,
                    split_balanced:         `Improvise the volatile-supply courses, structure anchor dishes that protect the margin`,
                    split_allfixed:         `Lock the whole menu as a fixed eight courses — promise consistency`,
                    split_allimprov:        `Improvise everything nightly — pure spontaneity, no fixed structure at all`,
                    r1_diagnosis_label:     `How rigorously does your team map cost, supply risk, waste and novelty per dish? (%)`,
                    r1_diagnosis_readout:   `Depth of the dish-by-dish tension map:`,

                    r2_sourcing_label:      `For the key ingredients, what is your dominant sourcing choice?`,
                    source_commit:          `Commit to the regenerative farms — full story value, higher cost, lower volume`,
                    source_blend_open:      `Blend regenerative with conventional, declared openly on the menu`,
                    source_blend_hidden:    `Blend conventional behind the regenerative claim — protect margin quietly`,
                    source_substitute:      `Substitute abundant local alternatives for the costly regenerative ingredients`,
                    r2_share_label:         `What regenerative share of the menu do you publicly claim? (%)`,
                    r2_share_readout:       `Claimed regenerative share:`,
                    r2_contingency_label:   `Contingency for the heirloom bean that may not arrive (designing for the 60% ceiling)?`,
                    cont_flexcourse:        `A “what the land gives” flex course that swaps to today's best harvest`,
                    cont_fixedprint:        `Print it as a fixed signature dish and hope the farm delivers`,
                    cont_lastminute:        `Buy conventional at the last minute if the harvest fails`,
                    r2_farmspend_label:     `Budget into farm volume guarantees and cold-chain logistics (€k)`,
                    r2_farmspend_hint:      `€k to secure weekly volume from the three farms and stabilise supply reliability.`,

                    r3_engineering_label:   `How do you apply the stars / plowhorses / puzzles / dogs grid?`,
                    eng_rebalance:          `Re-balance: promote high-margin stars, rework puzzles, cut the dogs`,
                    eng_keepall:            `Keep every creative dish — engineering kills the artistry`,
                    eng_marginonly:         `Keep only the cheapest dishes — pure margin, ignore novelty`,
                    r3_rootleaf_label:      `Root-to-leaf / whole-ingredient cooking — how much of the 9% waste do you convert to plated value? (%)`,
                    r3_rootleaf_readout:    `Trim, off-cuts and surplus turned into stock, ferments and garnish:`,
                    r3_joyless_label:       `The two dishes the panel called “worthy but joyless” — what do you do?`,
                    joy_rework:             `Rework them to be craveable AND regenerative — virtue must clear the desirability bar`,
                    joy_keep:               `Keep both unchanged — they are the most regenerative dishes`,
                    joy_cut:                `Cut both entirely and replace with conventional crowd-pleasers`,
                    r3_check_label:         `Average check after the relaunch (baseline €115)`,
                    r3_check_hint:          `€ per cover. Regulars notice a sharp jump — price can carry some cost, not all of it.`,

                    r4_flex_label:          `How does the launch menu flex weekly with the volatile harvest?`,
                    flex_anchorflex:        `Fixed anchor courses plus a weekly flex section — consistency and improvisation together`,
                    flex_fixedmenu:         `A fixed printed menu every week — promise the same eight courses`,
                    flex_totalimprov:       `Improvise the entire menu nightly with no fixed anchors`,
                    r4_story_label:         `How does front-of-house tell the regenerative story to guests?`,
                    story_woven:            `Woven lightly into the service — the food leads, the story supports`,
                    story_lecture:          `A detailed sustainability lecture with every course`,
                    story_silent:           `Say nothing — let guests discover it themselves`,
                    r4_model_label:         `What do you present to the critic and the award jury?`,
                    model_operating:        `A repeatable 12-month operating model with all four KPIs measured and owned`,
                    model_event:            `A dazzling one-night launch event — worry about month two later`,
                    model_margincase:       `A pure financial case — show the margin, downplay the regeneration claim`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you defend that constraints sharpened the creativity? (%)`,

                    warn_diagnosis_range:   `The depth of your tension map must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_share_range:       `The claimed regenerative share must be between 20% and 100%.`,
                    warn_farmspend_range:   `Farm and logistics spend must be between €0k and €60k.`,
                    warn_rootleaf_range:    `The share of waste you convert must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_check_range:       `The average check must be between €115 and €160.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_costnovelty:   `<strong>Right place to look.</strong> The collision isn't “creativity vs economics” everywhere — it is in the specific dishes that add cost without adding the novelty guests value. Flag those dish by dish and you have a target list, not a vague worry.`,
                    coach_r1_allcreative:   `<strong>The draft is dazzling — at 41% food cost.</strong> Your net margin is 9%; eight points of extra food cost wipes it out and pushes you into a loss within two quarters, right as the refit covenant tightens. Leone's chefs improvise <em>within</em> a structure. Where's yours?`,
                    coach_r1_cutregen:      `<strong>Strip out the regeneration and you've solved the wrong problem.</strong> You announced a regenerative relaunch to the press; gutting the soil-restoring ingredients to protect margin is the one move that loses both the star and the story. The discipline is engineering, not amputation.`,
                    coach_r1_priceonly:     `<strong>Price is not the whole answer.</strong> You're a one-star trattoria in the Bologna hills, not a three-star in the city. Lift the check too far and the regulars walk. Most of a 41% food cost has to be solved in the kitchen, not on the bill.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp read of the pantry.</strong> You located the cost-without-novelty dishes AND split the menu the way Leone's chefs do — improvise the volatile courses, structure the anchors that hold the margin. That is the scaffold that makes the improvisation productive instead of chaotic.`,
                    coach_r1_allfixed:      `<strong>A fixed eight courses against a 60% harvest is a promise you can't keep.</strong> Week three the heirloom beans don't arrive and you're 86-ing signature dishes in front of €115 guests. Regenerative supply is volatile by nature — improvisation here isn't optional.`,
                    coach_r1_allimprov:     `<strong>Pure spontaneity has no margin scaffold.</strong> Improvise everything and there's nothing structured to defend the food cost — that's how a brilliant kitchen runs itself into a loss. Leone's point is improvisation <em>within</em> structure, not instead of it.`,
                    coach_r1_shallow:       `<strong>A shallow tension map leaves you guessing.</strong> Without mapping cost, supply risk, waste and novelty dish by dish, every later decision is a hunch. The diagnosis is the cheapest leverage you have — spend on it now.`,
                    coach_r2_commit:        `<strong>Full commitment maxes the story — and the cost and volatility.</strong> Regenerative ingredients cost 22–60% more and the farms cover only 60% of volume. Beautiful for the jury, but you'll need engineering and a real flex plan to keep it solvent and supplied.`,
                    coach_r2_blendopen:     `<strong>An openly declared blend is the honest middle path.</strong> You protect some margin and reliability without lying about it — the menu says what's regenerative and what isn't, so the jury's audit finds exactly what you claimed. Integrity intact, P&L breathing.`,
                    coach_r2_blendhidden:   `<strong>Conventional beef behind a “pasture-raised” claim is not margin — it's a headline.</strong> The award jury audits your suppliers. When they find it, that's a greenwashing story in the regional press and your star is exposed. How much can you blend before the claim is a lie?`,
                    coach_r2_substitute:    `<strong>Abundant local substitutes are cheap and reliable</strong> — a legitimate move for the costliest, most volatile ingredients. Just watch the story and the novelty: substitute too much and the “regenerative” relaunch quietly becomes an ordinary seasonal menu.`,
                    coach_r2_overclaim:     `<strong>You claimed a regenerative share you can't actually back.</strong> A big public number with conventional product behind it is exactly what the jury's supplier audit catches. Either source it honestly or claim only what's true.`,
                    coach_r2_fixedprint:    `<strong>You printed the signature dish and crossed your fingers.</strong> The heirloom bean is the one ingredient that may simply not arrive. When it doesn't, you're 86-ing your headline course on a critic's night. Design the flex now, not in the weeds.`,
                    coach_r2_lastminute:    `<strong>Last-minute conventional buying breaks the claim quietly.</strong> The night the harvest fails, you swap in conventional product behind a regenerative menu — and if the jury samples that service, the story collapses. A declared flex course is the honest contingency.`,
                    coach_r2_nofarmspend:   `<strong>You under-funded the farm guarantees.</strong> Volume commitments and cold-chain logistics are what turn a 60% ceiling into reliable weekly supply. Starve that and you'll be improvising from shortage, not from abundance.`,
                    coach_r3_rebalance:     `<strong>That's menu engineering doing its job.</strong> Promoting high-margin stars, reworking the puzzles and cutting the dogs pulls food cost toward the floor while keeping the dishes guests actually order. Structure protects the margin so improvisation can protect the soul.`,
                    coach_r3_keepall:       `<strong>Keeping every creative dish is how the food cost stays at 41%.</strong> Engineering doesn't kill artistry — it funds it. Without re-balancing, the dazzling draft never comes inside the floor and the relaunch loses money beautifully.`,
                    coach_r3_marginonly:    `<strong>Cheapest-dishes-only is margin without a reason to visit.</strong> You'll clear the floor and gut the novelty and the regeneration in the same move — a Michelin guest doesn't drive into the hills for the cheap option. That's solving the P&L by deleting the restaurant.`,
                    coach_r3_rootleaf:      `<strong>Good — the trim is becoming a stock, a ferment, a garnish.</strong> Root-to-leaf cooking is the rare lever that cuts the waste KPI <em>and</em> the food cost at once. Push it further and the 9% waste turns into plated value instead of a disposal line.`,
                    coach_r3_engineered:    `<strong>This is the engineered menu the floor was waiting for.</strong> Re-balanced dishes plus aggressive root-to-leaf cooking pulls food cost and waste down together while novelty climbs. Constraint-driven creativity, exactly as Leone describes it.`,
                    coach_r3_wasteignored:  `<strong>You left the waste as a disposal line.</strong> That's €60K+ a year you're literally binning AND a broken ≤4% promise. Root-to-leaf cooking would cut the cost and the KPI in one move — why is the trim not a creative input?`,
                    coach_r3_keepjoyless:   `<strong>You kept the two dishes guests called “joyless” because they're virtuous.</strong> But customers come back for delight, not virtue — a Michelin guest who feels lectured doesn't rebook. Regeneration nobody wants to eat isn't sustainable as a business.`,
                    coach_r3_cutjoyless:    `<strong>Cutting them for conventional crowd-pleasers buys novelty by selling the claim.</strong> You removed your most regenerative dishes — the jury notices the relaunch just got less regenerative. The harder, better move was to rework them craveable, not delete them.`,
                    coach_r3_overpriced:    `<strong>You lifted the check past €140 to absorb the cost.</strong> Tidy on the spreadsheet — but your regulars notice a 22%+ jump overnight and volume falls. How much can price actually carry before the room empties, and what has to be solved in the kitchen instead?`,
                    coach_r4_anchorflex:    `<strong>Fixed anchors plus a weekly flex section is the answer to a volatile harvest.</strong> The anchors give guests consistency and hold the margin; the flex section absorbs whatever the farms actually deliver. Consistency and improvisation, designed to coexist.`,
                    coach_r4_fixedmenu:     `<strong>A fixed printed menu every week ignores the 60% ceiling.</strong> The harvest varies weekly; promise the same eight courses and you'll be buying conventional to fill the gaps or 86-ing dishes. The menu has to flex by design, not by emergency.`,
                    coach_r4_totalimprov:   `<strong>Total nightly improvisation thrills, but there's no margin scaffold.</strong> With no anchors, the food cost drifts and guests can't rely on the experience. Leone's chefs improvise within structure — keep the anchors that hold the floor.`,
                    coach_r4_lecture:       `<strong>A sustainability lecture with every course is how you make guests feel judged.</strong> The food should carry the story; a Michelin guest who wanted dinner and got a sermon doesn't rebook. Let the regeneration show on the plate, not in a speech.`,
                    coach_r4_silent:        `<strong>Saying nothing wastes the regeneration you paid for.</strong> If guests never learn why the menu changes weekly, the story — and the jury's interest — evaporates. Weave it lightly into the service; the food leads, the story supports.`,
                    coach_r4_operating:     `<strong>That's a relaunch the critic and jury can believe.</strong> A repeatable 12-month model with anchor-plus-flex sourcing, a lightly told story and all four KPIs owned proves regeneration is an operating model, not a one-night marketing event. The star is defensible.`,
                    coach_r4_event:         `<strong>A dazzling one-night event is the decay pattern.</strong> The critic eats once, the jury audits the system. Worry-about-month-two-later is how a relaunch slides back to a 41% draft the moment the cameras leave. Show the repeatable model.`,
                    coach_r4_margincase:    `<strong>Downplaying the regeneration to lead with margin contradicts your own announcement.</strong> You told the press this was a regenerative relaunch; pitching a pure financial case to the jury reads as a quiet retreat from the claim. The point was to prove both can hold.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
