                    sum_r3_good:            `Engineered the menu, reworked the joyless dishes, root-to-leaf waste cut.`,
                    sum_r3_overpriced:      `Solved a kitchen problem with the bill — regulars walk.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Menu not yet inside the floor without losing novelty.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Anchor-plus-flex, story woven, repeatable 12-month model owned.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Launch lacked a repeatable, defensible operating model.`,

                    insight_repeatable:     `Regenerative and repeatable: you brought food cost inside the 35% floor, cut waste to ≤4%, kept novelty craveable and protected the regenerative claim — and built a 12-month model the critic and jury can believe. Constraints sharpened the creativity rather than smothering it.`,
                    insight_balanced:       `You hit the margin floor, the waste target and the novelty bar together — a defensible relaunch. Lock in the anchor-plus-flex sourcing and the ownership so it survives past opening night.`,
                    insight_marginonly:     `You got food cost inside the floor, but at the expense of waste, novelty or the regenerative story. A solvent menu nobody craves — or nobody believes — isn't a sustainable relaunch.`,
                    insight_overcost:       `Food cost never came inside the 35% floor. The dazzling draft stayed dazzling and unprofitable; the 9% net margin flips negative within two quarters and the refit covenant tightens. Engineering, not artistry alone, was the missing discipline.`,
                    insight_greenwash:      `Greenwashed relaunch: you protected the P&L by blending conventional behind a regenerative claim the jury audits. That's not a margin win — it's a headline waiting to happen, and your Michelin star is exposed with it.`,
                    insight_floor:          `The 35% margin floor was breached at the buzzer — creativity ran ahead of the economics.`,
                    insight_waste:          `Food waste stayed above the ≤4% promise — the trim was treated as a disposal line, not a creative input.`,
                    insight_novelty:        `Novelty finished below the ≥80 target — the menu cleared the floor but lost the craveable delight guests return for.`,
                    insight_reliability:    `Supply reliability is fragile against the 60% ceiling — the menu will struggle to flex when the harvest dips.`,

                    hint_greenwash:         `You're blending conventional behind the regenerative claim — the jury audits suppliers. Either declare the blend openly or claim only what you can honestly source.`,
                    hint_cost:              `Food cost is still well above the 35% floor. Lean on menu engineering and root-to-leaf cooking, not just price, to bring it down.`,
                    hint_waste:             `Waste is still well above the ≤4% promise. Convert more of the trim into stock, ferments and garnish — it cuts the KPI and the food cost together.`,
                    hint_novelty:           `Novelty is slipping below what guests crave. Rework the joyless dishes and resist over-pricing — delight is what brings the regulars back.`,
                    hint_reliability:       `Supply reliability is low against the 60% ceiling. Fund the farm guarantees and design a flex course so a failed harvest doesn't break the service.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — inside the floor, waste on target and novelty craveable. Now lock in the anchor-plus-flex model and the KPI ownership so it lasts twelve months.`,

